  "id": "COMPOUND:8198 - PROTEIN:4911",
  "verbalization": "Patiromer is implicated in the regulation of CD276 antigen through a multi-step mechanism involving the vacuolar ATP synthase subunit S1. This pathway is supported by three distinct evidence chains, all of which point to the same target, suggesting a consistent biological role. The shared target of Patiromer, Vacuolar ATP synthase subunit S1, is a key player in cellular energy metabolism, and its interaction with multiple compounds—such as 5,7,8-Trihydroxy-4'-methoxyflavanone, Dimethyl Benzene-1,4-Dicarboxylate, and Ampelopsin A—indicates a broader pharmacological profile. The presence of a common target, CD276 antigen, across these pathways suggests that the vacuolar ATP synthase subunit S1 may serve as a critical mediator in the interaction between Patiromer and CD276. The integrated evidence supports a mechanistic link between Patiromer and CD276, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The presence of multiple compounds targeting the same subunit underscores the potential for a broader therapeutic application, provided the underlying molecular mechanisms are robustly characterized.",
